(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-comorbidities-contraindications-scores-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Comorbidities, Contraindications & Scores — Quiz 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 59-year-old man with a mitral annuloplasty ring is recovering from a stroke with severe dysphagia and cannot take anything by mouth. He has no drug allergies and needs a dental extraction. Which prophylaxis regimen fits?","choices":["Intravenous ampicillin","Amoxicillin by mouth","Doxycycline by mouth","Intravenous clindamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prosthetic material used for valve repair, such as an annuloplasty ring, qualifies for prophylaxis before an extraction. Without a penicillin allergy but unable to take oral medication, he gets ampicillin (intramuscular or intravenous), or cefazolin or ceftriaxone, 30 to 60 minutes before the procedure. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 35, 36"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man with atrial fibrillation, heart failure and chronic kidney disease is found at home with a cold left leg that has been painful for two days. The foot and calf have no sensation, he cannot move the ankle or toes, and the calf muscles are rigid. Neither arterial nor venous Doppler signals are audible. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography first","Immediate revascularization","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Primary major amputation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Profound sensory loss, paralysis with rigid muscles and inaudible arterial and venous Doppler signals define an irreversibly ischemic limb, Rutherford class III. Major tissue loss or permanent nerve damage is inevitable, and a nonviable extremity requires major amputation regardless of the therapy instituted. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 47, 62-67"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman had a ventricular septal defect repaired in childhood, with no residual defect. She now has a temperature of 38.3 °C (100.9 °F), and her long-known murmur sounds louder. One of three blood cultures grows coagulase-negative staphylococci. Transthoracic echocardiography shows no vegetation. How many modified Duke criteria does she meet?","choices":["No major, three minor","One major, two minor","One major, one minor","No major, one minor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Only her fever (38.0 °C or higher) counts, as one minor criterion. A repaired ventricular septal defect is excluded from predisposing conditions, an increase in a preexisting murmur is not sufficient for the major regurgitation criterion, and a single culture of coagulase-negative staphylococci is excluded as a common contaminant. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with metastatic lung cancer has worsening dyspnea. Her blood pressure is 84/52 mm Hg with pulsus paradoxus (a 10 mm Hg drop in blood pressure with inspiration), and her jugular venous pressure is elevated. Echocardiography shows a large effusion with right ventricular diastolic collapse. While pericardiocentesis is being arranged, which measure helps stabilize her?","choices":["Intravenous fluid bolus","Repeat echo tomorrow","Ibuprofen plus colchicine","Intravenous furosemide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulsus paradoxus, hypotension, raised jugular pressure and right ventricular diastolic collapse are cardiac tamponade, for which expedient pericardiocentesis is definitive. Intravenous fluids are given meanwhile to increase preload and prevent collapse of the right ventricle, a temporizing measure. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 59, 63, 68, 71"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and long-standing hypertension has a routine electrocardiogram. It shows a constant PR interval of 0.18 s with occasional P waves that are suddenly not followed by a QRS complex. He has no dizziness, fatigue or syncope. Which is the definitive management?","choices":["Observation; he has no symptoms","Transcutaneous pacing","Permanent pacemaker","Repeat tracing in a year"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A constant PR interval with randomly dropped QRS complexes is second-degree Mobitz type II block, common with degenerative conduction disease of old age, hypertension and diabetes. It is considered dangerous even without symptoms because it can progress to complete block and sudden cardiac death; definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 10, 13, 14"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, hypertension and a 40-pack-year smoking history has aching in both buttocks and hips when he walks, which stops with rest, and recent erectile dysfunction. Both femoral pulses are diminished. Which arterial segment is most likely diseased?","choices":["Aortoiliac segment","Common femoral artery","Tibial and peroneal arteries","Superficial femoral artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Buttock and hip claudication with diminished femoral pulses and erectile dysfunction is the Leriche triad of aortoiliac disease. His diabetes, hypertension and smoking explain the atherosclerosis; the pain's location and the weak femoral pulses locate it. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 14-16"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, hypertension and heart failure takes metoprolol, diltiazem and digoxin. She has had a week of fatigue but is alert and afebrile, with a blood pressure of 118/70 mm Hg and a normal neurologic examination. Her electrocardiogram shows a regular rhythm at 46 beats per minute with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Thyroid-stimulating hormone is 2.1 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0). Which is the most likely cause of her slow rhythm?","choices":["Late-stage infection","Hypothyroidism","Her rate-slowing medications","Raised intracranial pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular rate of 46 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS is sinus bradycardia, and beta blockers (metoprolol), nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (diltiazem) and digoxin are each a listed cause; she takes all three. Her normal thyroid result, normal neurologic examination and absent fever remove the thyroid, intracranial pressure and infection causes. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease is brought in confused and pale. His blood pressure is 74/40 mm Hg. The monitor shows a regular rhythm at 34 beats per minute with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Which is the first-line treatment?","choices":["Temporary pacemaker first","Permanent pacemaker now","Observation on the monitor","Intravenous atropine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is sinus bradycardia (normal P before each narrow QRS, rate under 60) that is acute, symptomatic and hemodynamically unstable: he is confused with a pressure of 74/40. For that situation atropine is first line, then a temporary pacemaker, with a permanent pacemaker kept for chronic symptomatic disease. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with type 2 diabetes had a mechanical aortic valve placed 8 months ago. He has 5 days of fever to 38.9 °C (102.0 °F) and a new regurgitant murmur. Three sets of blood cultures have just been drawn, and results are pending. Which empiric regimen fits?","choices":["Vancomycin, gentamicin, cefepime","Penicillin G plus gentamicin","Nafcillin plus gentamicin","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy, started only after blood cultures are drawn, covers staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci. For prosthetic valve endocarditis when the valve has been in place for 1 year or less, it is vancomycin plus gentamicin plus cefepime; his valve is 8 months old. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 17, 26, 27"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman with resistant hypertension has taken hydralazine for a year. She now has joint pains, pleuritic chest pain (sharp, worse with each breath in) and a pericardial friction rub (a superficial scratchy sound). Antinuclear antibody is positive, blood urea nitrogen is 14 mg/dL (normal 7-20), and she has had no tuberculosis exposure or recent viral illness. Which is the most likely cause of her pericarditis?","choices":["Uremic pericarditis","Tuberculous pericarditis","Viral pericarditis","Drug-induced lupus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Drug-induced lupus from procainamide or hydralazine is a listed iatrogenic cause of pericarditis. A year of hydralazine, joint pains and a positive antinuclear antibody fit it, and her normal urea nitrogen and lack of tuberculosis exposure or viral illness make the other causes unlikely. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 8, 11, 58"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with hemochromatosis, diabetes and liver disease has progressive dyspnea, ascites and bilateral leg edema, with right-sided failure dominating. Echocardiography shows normal ventricular size and wall thickness, a preserved ejection fraction, abnormal diastolic function and markedly enlarged atria. Which cardiomyopathy is most likely?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemochromatosis is a storage disorder that deposits iron in heart tissue and causes restrictive cardiomyopathy: stiff ventricles of normal size with a preserved ejection fraction, abnormal diastolic filling and marked atrial enlargement, with right heart failure often more prominent. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 36, 38, 41, 59, 72, 84-86, 96, 101"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with type 2 diabetes had a myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent 3 days ago and takes aspirin and clopidogrel. He now has sharp chest pain that is worse lying flat and eases when he leans forward, and a pericardial friction rub (a superficial scratchy or squeaking sound) is heard. Troponin is falling from its peak. Which anti-inflammatory drug is appropriate?","choices":["Aspirin","Ibuprofen","Indomethacin","Prednisone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Positional pain with a friction rub days after an infarction is post-infarction pericarditis. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen and indomethacin are not given for post-infarction pericarditis, and glucocorticoids are also avoided, so aspirin is the anti-inflammatory; he is already in the aspirin-as-antiplatelet group in whom the others are avoided. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 28, 29"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation takes clopidogrel after a coronary stent. Her blood pressure is 150/86 mm Hg, creatinine 1.0 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.2), liver tests are normal, she has had no stroke or bleeding, she is not on warfarin and she drinks two glasses of wine a week. What is her HAS-BLED (bleeding risk) score?","choices":["Score of 1","Score of 2","Score of 3","Score of 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Age over 65 scores 1 and clopidogrel (a medication predisposing to bleeding) scores 1: 1 + 1 = 2. Her pressure of 150 is below the uncontrolled line of above 160, her kidneys and liver are normal, and two drinks a week is far under the 8 or more drinks a week that would score. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis developed pericarditis 9 weeks ago. Despite treatment her sharp chest pain has never fully gone away; she has had no symptom-free stretch. Which term describes her pericarditis now?","choices":["Chronic","Incessant","Recurrent","Acute"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Incessant pericarditis is when symptoms persist for more than 4 to 6 weeks, up to 3 months, without a sustained remission. At 9 weeks with no symptom-free stretch she fits; past 3 months it would be chronic. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with ischemic heart disease and hypertension has palpitations. A monitor shows runs of three or more wide, identical beats lasting 6 to 10 seconds that stop on their own, without fainting or low blood pressure. His heart disease treatment is already optimized. Which is the first drug therapy?","choices":["Implantable defibrillator now","Intravenous magnesium","Synchronized cardioversion","Metoprolol or carvedilol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Three or more consecutive premature ventricular beats lasting under 30 seconds is non-sustained ventricular tachycardia, and symptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia is treated first with a beta blocker such as metoprolol or carvedilol; ablation or an antiarrhythmic follows if that fails. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 40, 43"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer had a large pericardial effusion drained for tamponade. Cytology of the fluid shows malignant cells. She is stable and asks what to expect next. Which step is recommended?","choices":["Pericardiectomy as a cure","No further follow-up needed","Palliative care consultation","Colchicine for 3 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion carries a limited life expectancy (median 2 to 4 months), and the effusion often recurs in that time. A palliative care consult is recommended to improve outcomes and quality of life, alongside close oncology and cardiology follow-up. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 69, 70"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with Marfan syndrome and hypertension has a 5.8 cm aneurysm of the ascending aorta on magnetic resonance imaging. He has no chest pain and no murmur of aortic regurgitation. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Repair once over 6.0 cm","Surgical repair","Imaging every 6 to 12 months","Imaging every 3 to 5 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Surgery is generally indicated for an ascending aortic aneurysm larger than 5.5 cm, and his measures 5.8 cm. An ascending aneurysm often needs an open approach with aortic root replacement, while beta blockers, blood pressure control and smoking cessation continue. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 85, 108-110"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with depression and chronic diarrhea was recently started on a medication known to prolong the QT interval. Her corrected QT is 560 ms, potassium 3.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5-5.0) and magnesium 1.4 mg/dL (normal 1.7-2.2). The monitor shows polymorphic ventricular tachycardia whose QRS complexes twist around the baseline (torsades de pointes). She is awake with a blood pressure of 112/70 mm Hg. Which drug is given first?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous metoprolol","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia twisting around the baseline with a long QT is torsades de pointes, and most torsades is acquired, from drugs, low potassium or low calcium. In a conscious patient intravenous magnesium breaks it, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 40, 42, 47"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with hypertension and stable angina ran out of his metoprolol 2 days ago. He feels his heart racing. His heart rate is 124 beats per minute with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. He is afebrile, hemoglobin is 14.8 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5), thyroid-stimulating hormone 2.0 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0) and oxygen saturation 98%. Which is the most likely cause of his tachycardia?","choices":["Underlying hypoxia","Metoprolol withdrawal","Underlying anemia","Hyperthyroidism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A normal P before every narrow QRS at 124 is sinus tachycardia, and abrupt withdrawal of medications such as beta blockers or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers is a listed cause. He stopped metoprolol 2 days ago, and his normal hemoglobin, thyroid test and oxygen saturation exclude anemia, hyperthyroidism and hypoxia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and peripheral artery disease had an ischemic stroke two years ago. She now has atrial fibrillation. She has no heart failure or hypertension. What is her CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score?","choices":["Score of 5","Score of 6","Score of 7","Score of 8"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age 75 or older scores 2, female sex 1, diabetes 1, peripheral artery disease (vascular disease) 1 and prior stroke 2: 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 7. She has no heart failure or hypertension to add. A score this high means she typically needs anticoagulation unless her bleeding risk is high. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy has a run of regular wide-complex tachycardia at 160 beats per minute on telemetry. Every QRS complex has the same shape. The run lasts 45 seconds and then stops; he stays alert with a normal blood pressure throughout. Which describes this rhythm?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Non-sustained tachycardia","Sustained monomorphic","Polymorphic tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three or more consecutive ventricular beats is ventricular tachycardia; a run lasting 30 seconds or more is sustained, and identical QRS shapes make it monomorphic. His 45-second run with the same shape every beat is sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, typical with cardiomyopathy. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 39, 40, 42"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with obesity and obstructive sleep apnea has had continuous atrial fibrillation for 5 weeks. It has not stopped on its own, and he and his clinician still plan to restore sinus rhythm. Which category describes his atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Paroxysmal","Long-standing persistent","Persistent","Permanent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation that is continuous for more than 7 days but less than 1 year is persistent and needs treatment. Five weeks of continuous rhythm fits that window, and since rhythm control is still planned it has not been declared permanent. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 46, 48"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman with paroxysmal atrial fibrillation and worsening chronic kidney disease takes digoxin. She has nausea and fatigue. Her electrocardiogram shows a regular rhythm at 48 beats per minute with narrow QRS complexes and retrograde P waves (inverted, after the QRS). Her digoxin level is 3.4 ng/mL (therapeutic 0.5-2.0). Which treatment fits?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Synchronized cardioversion","Digoxin immune Fab","No treatment needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular narrow rhythm at 48 with inverted P waves after the QRS is a junctional rhythm, and digoxin toxicity is a listed cause; her level of 3.4 is above the therapeutic range. Junctional rhythm from digitalis toxicity is treated with atropine and/or digoxin immune Fab (Digibind). — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 62, 64"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with hypertension, hyperlipidemia and type 2 diabetes had 30 minutes of left arm weakness three weeks ago that fully resolved. Duplex ultrasound shows 75% stenosis of the right internal carotid artery. He is otherwise fit for surgery. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Antiplatelet and statin therapy only","Carotid angioplasty with stenting","Repeat duplex in 6 months","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A transient ischemic attack within the last six months makes him symptomatic, and symptomatic stenosis over 70% is an indication for surgical intervention. He is fit for surgery, so carotid endarterectomy, the gold standard for high-grade stenosis, is preferred, with medical management of his comorbidities alongside. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 72, 74-77"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman who works rotating night shifts has a resting heart rate of 118 beats per minute with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Hemoglobin is 13.6 g/dL (normal 12-16), thyroid-stimulating hormone 1.8 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0), oxygen saturation 99%, and a drug screen is negative. She is mildly bothered by palpitations. Which is the first step in management?","choices":["Treat for hyperthyroidism","Remove influencing factors","Synchronized cardioversion","Long-term beta blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With anemia, thyroid disease, hypoxia and drugs excluded, this is inappropriate sinus tachycardia, a diagnosis of exclusion. Management starts by eliminating influencing factors, including lifestyle changes such as night shifts, medications and illicit drugs, plus behavioral therapy; it is generally left untreated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has had his lower leg in a plaster cast for 2 weeks after an ankle fracture. He comes to the emergency department with left calf pain, and his left calf measures 3.5 cm more than the right. He has obesity and hypertension. He has no pitting edema or deep-vein tenderness, and no other diagnosis is as likely. Which Wells likelihood category fits him?","choices":["Low likelihood","Moderate likelihood","High likelihood","Criteria do not apply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recent plaster immobilization of the leg 1 + calf swelling more than 3 cm 1 = 2, and totals of 1 to 2 are moderate likelihood. His obesity and hypertension are deep vein thrombosis risk factors, but they are not Wells items and do not push him into the high band. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 54-55"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy develops palpitations, and an electrocardiogram shows new atrial fibrillation. She also has hypothyroidism on replacement and osteoarthritis. Anticoagulation is started. Which medication is most appropriate to convert her rhythm and prevent recurrence?","choices":["Verapamil","Amiodarone","Digoxin","Metoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with atrial fibrillation, anticoagulation prevents embolic stroke, and amiodarone is very effective at converting atrial fibrillation and flutter to sinus rhythm and suppressing their recurrence. It also reduces the risk of sudden cardiac death, with or without outflow obstruction. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 76-78"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman who smoked for 30 years before quitting has hypertension, type 2 diabetes and a brother who had an abdominal aortic aneurysm repaired. Which feature of her history is associated with a DECREASED risk of abdominal aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Family history of aneurysm","Type 2 diabetes","Former smoking","Hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diabetes is associated with a decreased risk of abdominal aortic aneurysm, for reasons unknown, possibly diabetic treatment regimens or the arterial stiffness diabetes induces. Her female sex is protective too, while her past smoking, hypertension and family history all raise her risk. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 86-88"},{"q":"An 87-year-old frail woman with dilated cardiomyopathy, chronic kidney disease, recurrent falls and mild dementia has mild ankle edema and is comfortable at rest. Which approach to her medications best fits her age and frailty?","choices":["Rapid escalation of all drug classes","Left ventricular assist device","Aggressive diuresis to remove edema","Cautious diuretic and vasodilator use"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For elderly patients with cardiomyopathy, therapy is tailored to comorbidities and frailty, with cautious use of diuretics and vasodilators and a focus on symptom control and quality of life. Her falls, kidney disease and dementia are exactly the comorbidities that call for that caution. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 44, 105-106"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with knee osteoarthritis comes to the emergency department with sudden, severe pain behind the right knee that spread into the calf. She takes hormonal therapy for breast cancer that is still under treatment. Her right calf measures 4 cm more than the left, and she is tender along the deep veins. Her clinician judges a ruptured popliteal (Baker's) cyst at least as likely as a deep vein thrombosis. What is her Wells score?","choices":["Score of 1","Score of 2","Score of 3","Score of 5"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer 1 + calf swelling more than 3 cm 1 + deep-vein tenderness 1 = 3, then minus 2 because an alternative diagnosis, a ruptured popliteal (Baker's) cyst with its severe sudden calf discomfort, is at least as likely: 3 minus 2 = 1, the moderate band. Her osteoarthritis and age are not Wells items. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 52, 54-55"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man who smokes and has type 2 diabetes has a shallow, painful ulcer above the medial ankle, brown discoloration of the surrounding skin from hemosiderin and edema of the lower leg. His foot is cool, and the dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are faint. Which part of the usual venous ulcer plan should wait until arterial disease is ruled out?","choices":["Compression bandaging","Skin cleansing and emollients","Daily walking and ankle flexion","Referral to wound care"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Compression is the standard treatment for chronic venous insufficiency and its ulcers, but peripheral artery disease must be ruled out before it starts. A cool foot with faint pulses in an older smoker with diabetes calls for the arterial pulse examination and ankle-brachial index first. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 14-15, 28, 31-32, 35"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a first deep vein thrombosis of the left leg. He had no recent surgery, hospitalization, trauma, travel or leg injury, and he takes no hormones. His only conditions are gout and hypothyroidism, both stable for years. He is hemodynamically stable with no bleeding risk. How long should anticoagulation continue?","choices":["Indefinitely","3 months","Until symptoms resolve","3 to 6 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With no surgery, hospitalization, trauma, estrogen, travel or leg injury, this first venous thromboembolism has no identifiable provoking factor, so it is unprovoked. An unprovoked first event is treated for an indefinite length of time, beyond the 3-month minimum that applies to everyone. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 63-64"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with atrial fibrillation on warfarin and hypertension has had 1 day of sharp chest pain that is worse lying flat and better leaning forward. His electrocardiogram shows widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression. He is afebrile with stable vital signs, troponin is normal, and echocardiography shows a small effusion without tamponade. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Outpatient anti-inflammatories","Urgent pericardiocentesis","Discharge, recheck in a month","Hospital admission"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients on oral anticoagulants are among the features of higher risk and possible need for hospitalization in acute pericarditis, and anticoagulation is also linked to the poorest prognosis. His warfarin therefore moves him from outpatient care to admission even though he is otherwise stable. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 27, 32"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with no cardiac risk factors develops crushing chest pain and dyspnea an hour after learning her husband died. She has osteoporosis and hypothyroidism. Her electrocardiogram shows ST-segment elevation, troponin is elevated, and an echocardiogram shows hypokinesis of the apical and midsegments of the left ventricle. Which test confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Repeat troponin levels","Exercise echocardiography","Endomyocardial biopsy","Cardiac catheterization"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Takotsubo cardiomyopathy mimics acute coronary syndrome, with ST elevation, raised troponin and apical and midsegment hypokinesis, after a stressor such as a loved one's death in a postmenopausal woman. Cardiac catheterization confirms it by showing, most often, normal coronary arteries. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 55, 74, 101-104"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with hypertension has constrictive pericarditis caused by chest radiation he received for lymphoma years ago. He has an elevated jugular venous pressure that rises during inspiration (Kussmaul sign) and is scheduled for pericardiectomy. He asks what in his case most affects survival. Which feature is linked to the worst survival?","choices":["Prior chest radiation","Pericardial calcification","An idiopathic or viral cause","Choosing earlier surgery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Etiology is an important determinant of survival in constrictive pericarditis, and prior ionizing radiation is associated with the worst survival rates. Pericardiectomy still improves survival (5-year about 80%, 10-year about 60%), and earlier surgery has better outcomes. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 40, 47, 50"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man was treated for presumed heart failure with digoxin, and he also takes metoprolol, atorvastatin for hyperlipidemia and levothyroxine for hypothyroidism. An echocardiogram now shows asymmetric septal hypertrophy of 1.9 cm with a small left ventricular cavity and systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve (the anterior leaflet pulled into the outflow tract). Which medication should be stopped?","choices":["Metoprolol","Digoxin","Atorvastatin","Levothyroxine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The echocardiogram shows hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with obstruction: unexplained wall thickness of 1.5 cm or more, septal hypertrophy, a small cavity and systolic anterior motion. Digoxin is a positive inotrope, and positive inotropes are avoided because increased contractility raises the risk of myocardial ischemia and may induce arrhythmias. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 62, 72, 76-77"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with dilated non-ischemic cardiomyopathy (ejection fraction 25%) and chronic hypertension has worsening dyspnea on exertion despite medical therapy. His electrocardiogram shows a long-standing left bundle branch block (QRS 0.16 s, dominant S wave in V1, monophasic R waves in I, V5 and V6). Which treatment targets the block?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Biventricular pacemaker","No treatment for the block","Transcutaneous pacing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Left bundle branch block is usually left untreated, except when it is symptomatic with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, which calls for a biventricular pacer. His ejection fraction of 25% with exertional dyspnea fits that exception; dilated cardiomyopathy and hypertension are listed causes of the block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 24, 26, 27"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with hypertension controlled on lisinopril has a prior electrocardiogram showing pre-excitation (a short PR interval with a delta wave, a slurred upstroke of the QRS). She now has palpitations. She is alert with a blood pressure of 124/76 mm Hg, and the monitor shows a fast, irregularly irregular rhythm with wide, varying QRS complexes: atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. Which drug is the right choice?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous adenosine","Catheter ablation today","Intravenous diltiazem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her old tracing shows pre-excitation (short PR, delta wave), so this irregular wide rhythm is atrial fibrillation over an accessory pathway. Atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White is treated with intravenous procainamide, never adenosine; she is stable, so no shock is needed, and ablation of the pathway is the later definitive step. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 33, 35, 37, 52"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with osteoporosis and hypothyroidism develops chest pain and dyspnea in the hour after her house is destroyed by a hurricane. She has no hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia or smoking history. Her electrocardiogram shows ST-segment elevation, and her troponin is elevated. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Discharge with outpatient follow-up","Reassure that it is stress-related","Endomyocardial biopsy first","Treat as acute coronary syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her picture suggests takotsubo cardiomyopathy, a postmenopausal woman with few cardiac risk factors after a natural disaster, but it mimics acute coronary syndrome with no initial reliable differences. Evaluation and treatment therefore follow established acute coronary syndrome protocols, with admission to cardiology. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 55, 101-104"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has had poorly controlled hypertension for 25 years and also has chronic kidney disease. Echocardiography shows concentric left ventricular wall thickening of 1.6 cm. He has no family history of cardiomyopathy or sudden death. Which is the most likely explanation for the thickening?","choices":["Hypertensive heart disease","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is defined as left ventricular hypertrophy in the absence of a causative hemodynamic factor such as hypertension, and hypertensive heart disease is on its differential. Twenty-five years of poorly controlled hypertension explains his concentric thickening, even though it exceeds 1.5 cm. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 17, 38, 58, 70, 72, 84"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with a history of kidney stones, hypertension and heavy smoking has sudden severe left flank and back pain. His blood pressure is 84/50 mm Hg, a pulsatile abdominal mass is palpable, and there is flank ecchymosis (Grey Turner sign). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Renal colic from a stone","Diverticulitis","Ischemic bowel","Ruptured aortic aneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe abdominal or back pain, a pulsatile abdominal mass and hypotension are the classic presentation of rupture, and the pulsatile mass is virtually diagnostic. Flank ecchymosis (Grey Turner sign) marks retroperitoneal hemorrhage; his kidney stone history is the trap, since rupture is often misdiagnosed as renal colic. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 90-91"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy plays competitive rugby, drinks heavily on weekends and often restricts fluids before games to make weight. He has no symptoms. Which counseling is most appropriate?","choices":["Continue competitive rugby","Moderate noncompetitive activity","Avoid all physical activity","Restrict fluids before exertion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy have traditionally been told to keep to mild-to-moderate recreational activity in a noncompetitive way, because sudden cardiac death, the leading cause of death, is linked to extreme exertion. He should also stay hydrated and avoid excess alcohol. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 66, 69, 82"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis on immunosuppressive therapy has had 1 day of sharp chest pain that eases leaning forward. Her temperature is 38.7 °C (101.7 °F), troponin is 0.12 ng/mL (normal under 0.04), and echocardiography shows a small effusion without tamponade. She takes no anticoagulant and has had no trauma. How many of the listed higher-risk features for acute pericarditis does she have?","choices":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fever above 38 °C (1), immunosuppression (1) and an elevated troponin suggesting myopericarditis (1) are listed higher-risk features: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3. Her effusion is small (the feature is a MODERATE to large one), her onset was acute, and she has no anticoagulant or trauma. Any of these features points to possible hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 27"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with hypertension and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a 5.0 cm descending thoracic aortic aneurysm found on computed tomography. He has no chest or back pain, and the aneurysm has not grown over the past year. How often should it be imaged?","choices":["Imaging every 3 to 5 years","Surgical repair now","No further imaging","Imaging every 6 to 12 months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A 5.0 cm aneurysm of the descending thoracic aorta falls in the 4.5 to 5.4 cm band, imaged with computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging every 6 to 12 months. Repair is indicated for a descending aneurysm over 6.0 cm, growth over 0.5 cm per year or symptoms, and he has none. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 108-109"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy drinks about 10 beers a day and also has hypertension and gastroesophageal reflux. His echocardiogram shows dilated, thin-walled ventricles with an ejection fraction of 25%, and he has started guideline-directed medications. Which counseling point offers the best chance that his heart function will improve?","choices":["Sodium under 2 g a day","Maintain hydration","Stop alcohol entirely","Genetic counseling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Long-term excess alcohol is a cause of dilated cardiomyopathy, and cardiac function can recover if alcohol intake is reduced or halted. Removing the offending source is the step most able to improve his heart, alongside his medications. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 21, 23, 45, 69, 82"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and breast cancer has a peripherally inserted central catheter in her left arm for chemotherapy. She now has a fever of 39.4°C (102.9°F), a tender, indurated cord along the arm vein, redness spreading well beyond the vein's margin and pus at the insertion site. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Superficial phlebitis","Upper-extremity deep vein thrombosis","Superficial thrombophlebitis","Septic thrombophlebitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A tender, indurated cord along a superficial vein is thrombophlebitis; high fever, purulent drainage and redness extending well beyond the vein's margin point to infection within the vein. Septic thrombophlebitis is uncommon without venous cannulation, and her catheter supplies it. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 47, 51, 74-77"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease (creatinine 2.4 mg/dL, normal 0.6-1.2) has uncomplicated native mitral valve endocarditis from viridans streptococci and is responding quickly to therapy. A short combination course is being discussed. Which drug should be left out because of her kidney disease?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Penicillin G","Vancomycin","Gentamicin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For viridans streptococci, penicillin G or ceftriaxone can be given with gentamicin as a 2-week regimen, but only for uncomplicated endocarditis with a rapid response and NO chronic kidney disease. Her kidney disease rules out the gentamicin-containing short course. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man who had mitral valve surgery 6 months ago and has right-sided heart failure has palpitations. His electrocardiogram shows flutter waves (a sawtooth pattern) with 2:1 block and a ventricular rate of 150. He is alert with a blood pressure of 128/80 mm Hg. This is his third episode despite rate control. Which offers definitive management?","choices":["Pacemaker with defibrillator","Catheter ablation","Rate control by itself","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sawtooth flutter waves with 2:1 block are atrial flutter, common after atrial scarring from surgery and in right-sided heart failure. He is stable, rate control is harder in flutter and recurrence is substantial, as his third episode shows; ablation is the definitive management, with anticoagulation alongside. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 42, 44, 45"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with a prior myocardial infarction and type 2 diabetes has a regular wide-complex tachycardia at 190 beats per minute with identical QRS complexes. She has chest pain and a blood pressure of 82/50 mm Hg, and a carotid pulse is present. Which is the next step?","choices":["Unsynchronized defibrillation","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous lidocaine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Identical wide complexes at 190 are monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, and chest pain with hypotension makes it unstable. For unstable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia the next move is to check for a pulse: with a pulse present, synchronized cardioversion; without one, defibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 39, 45"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man who had infective endocarditis 2 years ago and has chronic kidney disease is scheduled for a screening colonoscopy. He has no active infection. Which is correct about endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Amoxicillin beforehand","Ampicillin beforehand","Azithromycin beforehand","Not recommended for this"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prior endocarditis puts him in a high-risk group, but prophylaxis is recommended only before dental procedures that manipulate the gums or tooth roots or perforate the oral mucosa, and invasive respiratory procedures. Gastrointestinal procedures such as colonoscopy do not need it in the absence of active infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 36"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man treated for infective endocarditis 3 years ago now has a temperature of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), conjunctival hemorrhages and Roth spots (white-centered retinal hemorrhages). One of three blood cultures grows viridans streptococci; the other two show no growth. Transesophageal echocardiography is normal. Which modified Duke classification fits?","choices":["Possible: one major, one minor","Possible: three minor criteria","Definite: five minor criteria","Definite: one major, three minor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prior endocarditis (predisposition), fever, conjunctival hemorrhages (vascular), Roth spots (immunologic) and one positive culture that does not meet the major rule (microbiologic) are five minor criteria: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5, which defines definite endocarditis without any major criterion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 12, 22, 23"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man who smokes and has hypertension reports calf pain after walking two blocks that eases with rest. Supine systolic pressures are: right arm 142 mm Hg, left arm 128 mm Hg; right ankle posterior tibial 92 and dorsalis pedis 86 mm Hg; left ankle posterior tibial 150 and dorsalis pedis 146 mm Hg. What is his overall ankle-brachial index?","choices":["0.61","0.65","0.72","1.06"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each leg's index is the higher of its two ankle pressures over the higher arm pressure: right 92/142 = 0.65, left 150/142 = 1.06. The lower of the two is the overall ankle-brachial index, 0.65, which sits in the claudication band of 0.4 to 0.9 and matches his calf pain with walking. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 27, 42-43"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with peripheral artery disease, type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease has sudden, tearing chest pain radiating to his back. Computed tomography confirms an aortic dissection. His blood pressure is 182/98 mm Hg. To what systolic range should his blood pressure be lowered now?","choices":["Systolic 80 to 100 mm Hg","Systolic 100 to 120 mm Hg","Systolic 120 to 130 mm Hg","Systolic 130 to 140 mm Hg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute aortic dissection is managed by stopping its progression: lower the systolic pressure to 100 to 120 mm Hg with a beta blocker such as esmolol or a vasodilator such as nitroprusside, with pain control and surgery. That acute target is far lower than his long-term goal for diabetes and kidney disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 50, 116-118"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with type 1 diabetes and a 15-pack-year smoking history has a preoperative electrocardiogram showing prominent Q waves in leads II, III, aVF, I, aVL and V4 through V6 with left axis deviation. He has never had chest pain, and echocardiography shows asymmetric septal hypertrophy of 2.0 cm with normal wall motion. What best explains the Q waves?","choices":["Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Prior silent myocardial infarction","Depolarization of a thickened septum","Left bundle branch block"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy the electrocardiogram can show prominent Q waves in the inferior leads (II, III, aVF) and lateral leads (I, aVL, V4 to V6) with left axis deviation, reflecting septal depolarization of hypertrophied myopathic tissue. His 2.0 cm asymmetric septum explains them. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 37, 72-73, 101, 103"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy has taken maximum medical therapy for 6 months, including an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, a beta blocker and spironolactone. He also has type 2 diabetes and gout, and his electrocardiogram shows no heart block. His ejection fraction is now 25% (normal 50% to 75%). Which device is indicated?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","No device; continue medications","Left ventricular assist device","Implanted defibrillator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is indicated when the ejection fraction stays below 35% after maximum medical therapy, as his 25% has. In dilated cardiomyopathy, sudden cardiac death from arrhythmia is a major reason for the 50% five-year mortality once symptoms emerge. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 5, 42-45, 99"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with migraine and hypothyroidism has just flown overseas. She has sudden shortness of breath, pleuritic chest pain and a swollen, tender left calf. Her electrocardiogram, normal last year, now shows a new right bundle branch block: QRS 0.14 s with an rsR' pattern in V1 to V3 (bunny ears, a second upward deflection) and a slurred S wave in I and aVL. Which is the most likely cause of the new block?","choices":["Pulmonary embolism","Age-related degeneration","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Lenègre-Lev disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A wide QRS with an rsR' (bunny ears) in V1 to V3 is right bundle branch block, and a sudden rise in right ventricular pressure from pulmonary embolism is a listed cause. Sudden dyspnea and pleuritic pain after a long flight point to it, and the block was absent a year ago. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 28, 29, 31"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man develops chest pain, palpitations and new heart failure. He has had no recent viral illness, and coronary angiography is normal. He takes methyldopa for hypertension, metformin for type 2 diabetes, atorvastatin for hyperlipidemia and omeprazole for reflux. Myocarditis is suspected. Which of his medications is a recognized cause?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Methyldopa","Omeprazole","Metformin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Methyldopa is among the medications that can cause myocarditis, along with penicillin, thiazide diuretics and tetracycline. With no viral prodrome and normal coronaries, a drug cause deserves attention. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 49, 54"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man had a myocardial infarction treated with a stent four years ago and has hypertension. He now has atrial fibrillation. He has no heart failure, diabetes, stroke or transient ischemic attack. What is his CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score?","choices":["Score of 1","Score of 2","Score of 3","Score of 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age 66 is in the 65-74 band for 1 point, the prior myocardial infarction counts as vascular disease for 1, and hypertension adds 1: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3. Male sex scores 0, and he has no heart failure, diabetes or prior stroke. He is above the low-risk line of 0 for a man. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation is on hemodialysis for end-stage kidney disease, takes daily aspirin for a coronary stent and had a major gastrointestinal bleed last year. His blood pressure is 148/84 mm Hg. He has no liver disease, prior stroke or unstable international normalized ratio, and he drinks no alcohol. What is his HAS-BLED (bleeding risk) score?","choices":["Score of 2","Score of 3","Score of 4","Score of 5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age over 65 scores 1, renal disease on dialysis 1, prior major bleeding 1 and aspirin (medication predisposing to bleeding) 1: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4. His systolic pressure of 148 does not reach the uncontrolled line of above 160, so hypertension adds nothing. This score estimates his risk of major bleeding. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with sepsis and ongoing gastrointestinal bleeding becomes unresponsive. The monitor shows an organized narrow-complex rhythm at 90 per minute, but no pulse can be felt. Which is the next step?","choices":["Transcutaneous pacing","Synchronized cardioversion","Compressions and epinephrine","Immediate defibrillation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An organized rhythm that should produce a pulse but does not is pulseless electrical activity, and its two leading causes in adults are hypovolemia and hypoxia; bleeding makes hypovolemia likely here. It is unshockable: start chest compressions immediately, with epinephrine every 3 to 5 minutes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 52, 55"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with long-standing varicose veins of both legs comes to urgent care with swelling of her entire left leg for two days. She is receiving palliative treatment for metastatic breast cancer. Her left calf measures 2 cm more than the right, and she has no pitting edema and no tenderness along the deep veins. No other diagnosis is as likely. What is her Wells score for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Score of 1","Score of 2","Score of 3","Score of 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer, which includes palliation within 6 months, 1 + entire leg swollen 1 = 2, the moderate band. Her varicose veins do not score, because the item is collateral superficial veins that are nonvaricose, and a 2 cm calf difference falls short of the more-than-3 cm cutoff. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 54-55"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman comes to urgent care with right calf aching that began after a 10-hour flight four days ago. She takes an estrogen-containing oral contraceptive and smokes. Her right calf measures 3.5 cm more than the left 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity, and she is tender along the deep veins of the calf. She has no pitting edema, the whole leg is not swollen, and no other diagnosis is as likely. What is her Wells score for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Score of 2","Score of 3","Score of 4","Score of 5"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Calf swelling more than 3 cm over the other leg 1 + localized tenderness along the deep venous system 1 = 2, the moderate band of 1 to 2. The flight, the contraceptive and smoking are all deep vein thrombosis risk factors, but none is a Wells item: immobility scores only as being bedridden more than 3 days, major surgery or a recent cast. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 49-50, 54-55"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with peripheral artery disease and calf claudication also has renal failure and gout. Her blood pressure averages 146/88 mm Hg on one drug. Which blood pressure goal applies to her?","choices":["Below 120/80 mm Hg","Below 130/80 mm Hg","Below 140/90 mm Hg","Below 150/90 mm Hg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In peripheral artery disease the antihypertensive goal is below 140/90 mm Hg, tightened to below 130/80 mm Hg for patients with diabetes or renal failure. Her renal failure puts her on the stricter goal, so her current 146/88 needs more treatment. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve and type 2 diabetes has 10 days of malaise and fever to 38.8 °C (101.8 °F). Two separate blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. Transesophageal echocardiography shows an oscillating mass on the prosthetic valve. Which modified Duke classification fits?","choices":["Definite: one major, three minor","Possible: one major, one minor","Definite: two major criteria","Possible: three minor criteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus, a typical organism, from two separate blood cultures is one major criterion, and an oscillating intracardiac mass on the valve is the second (evidence of endocardial involvement). Two major criteria define definite endocarditis; his prosthetic valve and fever are minor criteria that are not needed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a recent large anterior myocardial infarction and type 2 diabetes develops sudden severe pain in his right leg. Two hours later the foot is cold and pale with absent pulses, numbness extends above the toes, and he has moderate weakness of ankle dorsiflexion. The arterial Doppler signal is inaudible; the venous signal is audible. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography first","Primary major amputation","Intravenous heparin and observation","Revascularization in the surgical suite"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sensory loss beyond the toes with moderate weakness and an inaudible arterial but audible venous signal is Rutherford IIB, immediately threatened: salvageable only with immediate revascularization, so he should go to the surgical suite. A left ventricular thrombus after a myocardial infarction is a classic cardiac source of the embolus. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 60-66"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with peripheral artery disease, hypertension and hyperlipidemia has calf claudication after one block. He quit smoking and takes aspirin and a statin, but he has never done an exercise program. He asks about having his artery opened. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Supervised exercise program","Endovascular angioplasty now","Bypass graft surgery","Home-based walking program"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Revascularization is recommended for claudication with severe functional disability only after an inadequate response to exercise or drug therapy, and he has never tried exercise. A supervised program, more successful than a home program, of at least 30 to 45 minutes three times a week improves walking distance. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 50, 53, 57"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has a newly diagnosed deep vein thrombosis that extends into the iliac and femoral veins. She is hemodynamically stable, has normal kidney function, has never had a bleeding problem and is reliable with follow-up. She also has treated hypothyroidism and takes an estrogen-containing contraceptive. Which finding makes outpatient treatment inappropriate?","choices":["Iliofemoral extent of the clot","Estrogen-containing contraceptive","Treated hypothyroidism","First episode of thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient therapy is not appropriate for an iliofemoral (proximal) deep vein thrombosis, even in a patient who is otherwise stable, at low bleeding risk, free of renal insufficiency and reliable. Her other features all fit outpatient care; the location of the clot is what sends her to inpatient treatment. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 63-65"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a bioprosthetic aortic valve is receiving chemotherapy and cannot keep any pills down. His penicillin allergy was a mild, delayed rash years ago, with no hives, swelling or breathing trouble. He needs a dental extraction. Which prophylaxis regimen fits?","choices":["Azithromycin by mouth","Intravenous clindamycin","Intravenous cefazolin","Intravenous ampicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A prosthetic valve qualifies for prophylaxis before an extraction. A penicillin-allergic patient who cannot take oral medication gets cefazolin or ceftriaxone, and since his reaction was a mild rash without anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria, a cephalosporin is allowed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 35, 36"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation has daily palpitations. Monitoring shows frequent premature atrial contractions (early, abnormally shaped P waves), and his atrial fibrillation episodes start right after runs of them. An echocardiogram shows no structural heart disease. He has cut out caffeine and alcohol, and three months of metoprolol has not helped. Which treatment fits best?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Look for structural disease","Reassurance alone","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Premature atrial contractions that persist with symptoms are treated with a beta blocker if not contraindicated, or with catheter ablation when they are frequent or are triggering atrial fibrillation. His are frequent, trigger his atrial fibrillation and did not respond to metoprolol, so ablation is the fit. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 54, 58"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is admitted with a bleeding duodenal ulcer that is still oozing on endoscopy. On day 2 his right thigh swells, and duplex ultrasound shows a clot in the femoral vein. He also has hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Which is the most appropriate management of the clot?","choices":["Rivaroxaban monotherapy","Warm compresses and elevation","Inferior vena cava filter","Full-dose enoxaparin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Active bleeding is a contraindication to anticoagulants. An inferior vena cava filter is used only when there is an acute proximal deep vein thrombosis of the leg, such as a femoral clot, together with active bleeding or another contraindication to anticoagulation, which is exactly his situation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 48, 62, 69, 78-79"}]};
